# Erdos Problem #99 - low-t singleton leaf-tooth gate

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Target:** C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch
**Dependencies:** q-k amplified bridge-subtracted train split; low-t pentagon sea; empty-pentagon trapezoid stability; Wegner first residual jump
**Claim class:** proof-only closing reduction awaiting independent audit
**External literature:** no new import

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The low-`t` endpoint contains much more exceptional singleton-train mass than the
previous singleton-anchored Groemer gate uses.  Near the current `t_42` floor the
high-degree face budget `X:=Q-f` is only about `0.002884D`.  Jointly charging all
singleton incidences that can avoid the four leaf-tooth sides of a cheap pentagon
leaves a large forced family of two-contact teeth with a singleton-train side.

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},\qquad
C_0:=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00042,
\]

\[
t_*:={4\over9}(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2})+0.0013751,
\qquad
L_*:=t_*+0.00084.
\]

For a scalar upper endpoint `t_0`, define

\[
A_0(t_0):={9t_0\over4}-\pi-{C_0\over2},
\tag{SL.1}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
G(t_0):=L_*-8A_0(t_0)-4t_0+2\pi
      =L_*-22t_0+10\pi+4C_0.
}
\tag{SL.2}
\]

Fix `eta>0`.  There is `theta_eta>0` such that every pentagonal exceptional face
with residual below `theta_eta` is isolate-free and close enough to the flat
trapezoid that both leaf hidden chords have length in `(1,1+eta)`.

Let `A_eta^leaf` be the maximum number of pairwise vertex-disjoint leaf teeth from
such faces for which at least one of the two exact unit sides is a singleton stopped
train.  Then every Type-II subsequence with

\[
\limsup {q-k\over D}\le t_0
\]

satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\sigma_R+104A_\eta^{leaf}+{8\over\theta_\eta}R_F
\ge (G(t_0)-o(1))D.
}
\tag{SL.3}

At `t_0=t_*`,

\[
\boxed{G(t_*)=0.7982875932\ldots .}
\tag{SL.4}
\]

Hence on `R_F=o(D)`,

\[
\boxed{
\sigma_R+104A_\eta^{leaf}
\ge(0.7982875932\ldots-o(1))D.
}
\tag{SL.5}
\]

Without any further branch choice this already gives

\[
\boxed{
\max\left\{{\sigma_R\over D},{A_\eta^{leaf}\over D}\right\}
\ge0.00760273898\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{SL.5a}
\]

More generally, for any fixed nonnegative `s,r`, if along the same subsequence

\[
\limsup {\sigma_R\over D}\le s,
\qquad
\limsup {R_F\over\theta_\eta D}\le r,
\]

then

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {A_\eta^{leaf}\over D}
\ge {\bigl(G(t_*)-s-8r\bigr)_+\over104}.
}
\tag{SL.6}
\]

Thus (SL.5a), rather than the now-excluded hypothetical branch `sigma_R=o(D)`, is the
useful small-residual consequence when this theorem is composed with the independent
positive rhombic-slack floor.

Without assuming `R_F=o(D)`, put `r_\eta:=R_F/(\theta_\eta D)`. Equation (SL.3)
also gives the three-way normalized alternative

\[
\boxed{
\max\left\{{\sigma_R\over D},{A_\eta^{leaf}\over D},r_\eta\right\}
\ge {G(t_*)\over113}-o(1)
=0.0070644\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{SL.6a}
\]

The coefficient `G(t_0)` remains positive until

\[
t_0<{L_*+10\pi+4C_0\over22}=1.857\ldots .
\tag{SL.7}
\]

Thus the gate covers almost the whole old `t<=1.8585` endpoint band.

---

## 1. Amplified exceptional-singleton currency

Write

\[
X:=Q-f,\qquad
\delta_D:={b-P_F\over D},\qquad
\kappa_D:={k\over D}.
\]

Let `S_E` count singleton stopped trains with at least one bounded exceptional-face
terminal incidence.  The q-k amplified bridge-subtracted theorem gives

\[
\boxed{
\sigma_R+2S_E+(b-P_F)\ge(L_*-o(1))D.
}
\tag{SL.8}

The low-`t` face ledger gives, on `limsup t_D<=t_0`,

\[
\boxed{
{X\over D}
\le A_0(t_0)-\delta_D-{\kappa_D\over4}+o(1).
}
\tag{SL.9}

No split into pentagonal and nonpentagonal singleton trains is made yet.

---

## 2. Exact capacity of singleton incidences that avoid leaf teeth

For every train counted by `S_E`, choose one bounded exceptional-face terminal
incidence.  If the train touches a pentagon, choose a pentagonal incidence; otherwise
choose any bounded exceptional incidence.

Let `n_d` be the number of bounded exceptional faces of degree `d>=5`, and put

\[
N_{\ge6}:=\sum_{d\ge6}n_d,
\qquad
p_5:=n_5.
\]

The degree-excess identities are

\[
f=p_5+N_{\ge6},
\qquad
X=Q-f=\sum_{d\ge6}(d-5)n_d.
\tag{SL.10}

Every chosen singleton assignment landing on a nonpentagonal face uses a distinct
physical boundary edge, so the total number of such assignments is at most

\[
\sum_{d\ge6}d n_d
=5N_{\ge6}+X.
\tag{SL.11}

On a cheap pentagon use the flat-trapezoid cyclic labeling

\[
l,m,r,a,b.
\]

The two leaf teeth are `(m,l,b)` and `(m,r,a)`.  Their exact unit sides are

\[
lm,\quad bl,\quad mr,\quad ra,
\]

while `ab` is the unique boundary edge not belonging to either leaf tooth.  Thus at
most one chosen assignment on a cheap pentagon can avoid all leaf-tooth sides.
Across all pentagons this nominal bad-edge capacity is at most `p_5`.

The number of pentagons with residual at least `theta_eta` is at most
`R_F/theta_eta`.  A high-residual pentagon may invalidate the leaf geometry on its
other four edges; after the one nominal bad-edge slot is already charged, this costs
at most four additional assignments per such face.  Therefore the number `G_E` of
chosen singleton assignments that land on a leaf-tooth side of a low-residual
pentagon obeys

\[
\begin{aligned}
G_E
&\ge S_E-\left(5N_{\ge6}+X+p_5\right)
          -{4R_F\over\theta_\eta}\\
&=S_E-\left(Q+4N_{\ge6}\right)
          -{4R_F\over\theta_\eta}\\
&\ge
\boxed{
S_E-Q-4X-{4R_F\over\theta_\eta},
}
\tag{SL.12}
\end{aligned}

because `N_{>=6}<=X`.

This joint charge is sharper than separately paying `6X` for nonpentagonal singleton
incidences and one full pentagon count for bad pentagon edges.

---

## 3. Extract vertex-disjoint singleton leaf teeth

A cheap pentagon has four leaf-tooth sides, so at least `G_E/4` distinct pentagons
receive a good singleton assignment.  From each such pentagon choose one of its two
leaf teeth containing the assigned singleton side.

There is now at most one chosen triple per pentagon.  In a triangle-free unit-contact
graph every source vertex has contact degree at most five, hence is incident with at
most five bounded pentagonal face sectors.  The chosen-triple conflict graph therefore
has maximum degree at most

\[
3(5-1)=12.
\]

A greedy independent set retains at least one thirteenth.  Hence

\[
\boxed{
52A_\eta^{leaf}
\ge S_E-Q-4X-{4R_F\over\theta_\eta}.
}
\tag{SL.13}

Equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
104A_\eta^{leaf}+{8R_F\over\theta_\eta}
\ge2S_E-2Q-8X.
}
\tag{SL.14}

No finite shear of the singleton edge is used.  In particular, the singleton need not
be a bridge.

---

## 4. Insert the low-t ledgers

Combine (SL.14) with (SL.8):

\[
\sigma_R+104A_\eta^{leaf}+{8R_F\over\theta_\eta}
\ge L_*D-(b-P_F)-2Q-8X-o(D).
\tag{SL.15}

The exact outer ledger is

\[
{Q\over D}=2t_D-\pi-\delta_D.
\tag{SL.16}

Using `t_D<=t_0+o(1)` and (SL.9), the right side of (SL.15), divided by `D`, is at
least

\[
\begin{aligned}
&L_*-\delta_D-2(2t_0-\pi-\delta_D)
 -8\left(A_0(t_0)-\delta_D-\kappa_D/4\right)-o(1)\\
&=L_*-8A_0(t_0)-4t_0+2\pi
  +9\delta_D+2\kappa_D-o(1)\\
&\ge G(t_0)-o(1).
\end{aligned}
\]

This proves (SL.3).

---

## 5. Metric gap on the extracted family

The pentagon hidden-gap theorem gives more than pointwise convergence.  For every
fixed `lambda<2/3`, after shrinking the cheap-pentagon threshold if necessary, the
sum of the two hidden leaf gaps over any family of retained pentagons is at most
`R_F/lambda`.

The family extracted above is a subset of those leaf teeth.  Hence it can be chosen so
that

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{(x,y,z)\in A_\eta^{leaf}}(|x-z|-1)
\le {R_F\over\lambda}.
}
\tag{SL.17}

On `R_F=o(D)`, a slow diagonal choice `lambda_D->2/3` and `eta_D->0` yields

\[
\sum_{A_{\eta_D}^{leaf}}(|x-z|-1)
\le\left({3\over2}+o(1)\right)R_F=o(D).
\tag{SL.18}

Thus the hard low-`t`, low-residual, low-rhombic-slack corner contains linearly many
vertex-disjoint triples with two exact unit contacts, one of those contacts a
singleton train, and only `o(D)` aggregate hidden-contact gap across the whole family.

---

## 6. Scope audit

- `S_E` counts singleton trains, not incidences.  Exactly one bounded exceptional
  incidence is selected per train.
- A nonpentagonal face of degree `d` absorbs at most `d` chosen assignments.  The exact
  sum of these capacities is `5N_{>=6}+X`.
- The combined bad-incidence capacity is `Q+4N_{>=6}<=Q+4X`; this is the key sharpening.
- A high-residual pentagon pays only four extra slots because the one generic bad-edge
  slot has already been charged through `p_5`.
- The factor `52=4*13` consists of four possible leaf-side assignments per pentagon and
  the degree-12 one-triple-per-face conflict extraction.
- The theorem does not assert that a pentagon-touching singleton edge is a bridge and
  does not invoke the audited bridge-only singleton shear.
- No KKT multiplier is assigned to a hidden chord.  No exact unit triangle or strict
  same-cardinality sub-`D` source is produced.

**Disposition:** proof-only low-`t` closing reduction.  C-II and Level I remain open.
